<title>USA: U.S. Feb wholesale inventories unchanged.</title>
<headline>U.S. Feb wholesale inventories unchanged.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-04-08</dateline>
<text>
<p>Commerce Department report of wholesale inventories and sales, seasonally adjusted.</p>
<p> (Percent Changes)	Feb    Jan--Rev from  Feb97/96</p>
<p> Inventories Total	UNCH   0.8     1.0	1.5</p>
<p> Sales Total		2.1    0.8     1.0	8.5</p>
<p> (Billions of dollars)    Feb     Jan--Rev from   Feb96</p>
<p> Inventories Total   260.05  260.10  260.80   256.19</p>
<p> -Durable Goods	164.45  164.35  164.64   162.75</p>
<p> --Cars/Parts	   26.65   27.44   27.71    27.85</p>
<p> -Non-Durable Goods   95.60   95.75   96.16    93.44</p>
<p> --Petroleum Prods.    5.71    5.73    5.73     5.06</p>
<p> (Billions of dollars)    Feb     Jan--Rev from   Feb96</p>
<p>  Sales Total	  209.67  205.40  205.79   193.33</p>
<p> -Durable Goods	107.32  104.50  104.76   100.01</p>
<p> --Cars/Parts	   17.63   17.51   17.51    17.12</p>
<p> -Non-Durable Goods  102.35  100.90  101.03    93.32</p>
<p> --Petroleum Prods.   16.77   17.40   17.51    14.18</p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast February wholesale inventories to rise 0.4 percent.</p>
<p>The February stock-to-sales ratio for merchant wholesalers, seasonally adjusted, fell to 1.24 months' worth, matching a lowpoint last reached on September 1984, from 1.27 months' worth in January, the department said.</p>
<p>The ratio is a measure of how many months it would take to sell existing inventories at the current sales pace.</p>
<p>The 2.1 percent jump in overall February sales was the biggest increase since a 2.7 percent rise in August 1994, the department said.</p>
